// A statement continues across a newline when the **next line starts** with an infix operator — the
// mirror of `trailing_operator_continuation.noe`, where the operator ends the line instead. Both
// spellings are the same program, and every infix operator supports both.
//
// The rule is that a newline ends a statement unless the following line's first token continues it,
// and an infix operator continues it because it cannot begin one. `~`, `<<`, `&` and `^` are the
// operators worth pinning: they read as a group with a prefix meaning and have none — Noeta spells
// complement `!`, not `~` — so a line beginning with one of them has exactly one reading.
//
// This is also the corpus gate on the `noeta fmt` printer from the other side. Under `wrap = true`
// the printer breaks a wide binary chain before its operator, producing precisely this shape; when
// one of these four did not continue the line, that output lexed as two statements and the
// formatter's own re-parse check refused to format the file at all.
// expect: stdout "abc"
// expect: stdout "2"
// expect: stdout "5"
// expect: stdout "16"
// expect: stdout "11"
// expect: stdout "one-two"
// expect: exit 0

// Leading `~`: the concatenation continues from the line above.
joined = "a"
    ~ "b"
    ~ "c"
echo joined

// The bitwise trio reads the same way.
echo 10
    & 6
echo 6
    ^ 3
echo 1
    << 4

// Mixed with an operator that always worked, in one chain — `>>` is two `>` tokens rather than one
// `>>`, which is why it kept working when `<<` did not, and the two must now behave alike.
echo 1
    << 4
    >> 1
    | 3

// Indented across several lines, the shape a wrapped real program writes.
label = "one"
    ~ "-"
    ~ "two"
echo label
